  "critical": false,
  "critical_note": "Not on the minimum-viable path: radioisotope power requires a operating nuclear reactor to produce Pu-238/Sr-90/Am-241, plus hot-cell fuel fabrication and thermoelectric semiconductor manufacturing — capabilities that arrive decades into the industrial buildup",
  "description": "Conversion of radioactive decay energy into electricity and heat. Alpha-emitting Pu-238 and beta-emitting Sr-90 sources are encapsulated in ceramic fuel and coupled to thermoelectric, Stirling, thermophotovoltaic, or direct-charge conversion stages. Delivers 1-300 W of unattended power for 10-100 years with no refueling, enabling deep-space missions, polar sensors, and implanted medical devices. Requires isotope production via reactor irradiation, hot-cell fuel fabrication, and multi-layer containment qualified for launch accidents.",
  "early_win": false,
